1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,168
How do you stop a website taking more money than it is allowed?

2
00:00:03,218 --> 00:00:08,688
In Lesson 21, each website's deal set the most it may take per payment and per day.

3
00:00:08,738 --> 00:00:13,344
In Lesson 27, one claim made sure a card was charged only once.

4
00:00:13,394 --> 00:00:18,480
This lesson makes those limits hold, even when five buyers pay at the same moment.

5
00:00:18,530 --> 00:00:20,352
Two things can break a limit.

6
00:00:20,402 --> 00:00:25,608
A website's account is taken over, and the attacker creates one payment for a fortune.

7
00:00:25,658 --> 00:00:29,136
Or, on a busy day, five buyers pay at once.

8
00:00:29,186 --> 00:00:33,744
Each payment fits the day's limit alone, but together they go far over it.

9
00:00:33,794 --> 00:00:41,832
A check that first reads the day's total and then charges lets all five through, because all five read the total before any of them is added.

10
00:00:41,882 --> 00:00:44,520
Think of a concert hall with one seat left.

11
00:00:44,570 --> 00:00:49,056
Two clerks sit at the counter, and two fans arrive at the same moment.

12
00:00:49,106 --> 00:00:51,744
The hall still sells that seat only once.

13
00:00:51,794 --> 00:00:54,408
Whoever comes second hears: sold out.

14
00:00:54,458 --> 00:00:57,288
A website's payment limits work the same way.

15
00:00:57,338 --> 00:01:02,784
Payment limits are the most a website may take in one payment, and the most in one day.

16
00:01:02,834 --> 00:01:06,528
The bank sets both for each website, as part of its deal.

17
00:01:06,578 --> 00:01:08,400
The first limit is simple.

18
00:01:08,450 --> 00:01:12,384
A payment over the most per payment is refused the moment it is created.

19
00:01:12,434 --> 00:01:16,824
That holds whether the merchant's server creates it, or a payment link does.

20
00:01:16,874 --> 00:01:20,376
The daily limit is harder, because buyers race each other.

21
00:01:20,426 --> 00:01:23,208
So each charge makes an atomic reservation.

22
00:01:23,258 --> 00:01:26,160
Atomic means one step that cannot be split.

23
00:01:26,210 --> 00:01:33,192
In that one step, the charge adds its amount to the day's total, but only if the new total still fits under the limit.

24
00:01:33,242 --> 00:01:37,632
When two charges arrive together, one waits until the other is done.

25
00:01:37,682 --> 00:01:41,448
The one that no longer fits fails before any money moves.

26
00:01:41,498 --> 00:01:47,568
The reservation is saved together with the charge's own record, before the card network is ever asked.

27
00:01:47,618 --> 00:01:50,784
If the card is declined, its share goes back to the day.

28
00:01:50,834 --> 00:01:53,712
A reversal on the same day gives it back too.

29
00:01:53,762 --> 00:01:58,824
A payment whose outcome is unknown keeps its share until someone knows what happened.

30
00:01:58,874 --> 00:02:02,448
And the day is the same business day the daily reports add up.

31
00:02:02,498 --> 00:02:09,216
In front of all of this stands the edge: the first server every request meets when it arrives from the internet.

32
00:02:09,266 --> 00:02:12,552
It accepts only modern encrypted connections.

33
00:02:12,602 --> 00:02:15,792
It tells browsers to use secure connections only.

34
00:02:15,842 --> 00:02:17,928
It never names the software it runs.

35
00:02:17,978 --> 00:02:23,232
It refuses requests that are too large, and it limits how fast one address may call.

36
00:02:23,282 --> 00:02:25,104
Let's watch the product.

37
00:02:25,154 --> 00:02:30,216
A bank admin opens a website in the bank's portal and goes to its deal terms.

38
00:02:30,266 --> 00:02:34,296
Two fields sit side by side: Most per payment, and Most per day.

39
00:02:34,346 --> 00:02:37,056
A hint says that empty means no limit.

40
00:02:37,106 --> 00:02:41,040
The admin enters the bank's chosen amounts and saves.

41
00:02:41,090 --> 00:02:47,328
In the merchant's portal, the website now shows its limits: so much a payment, so much a day.

42
00:02:47,378 --> 00:02:51,600
The merchant creates a payment link for an amount above the per-payment limit.

43
00:02:51,650 --> 00:02:54,336
The portal shows a red notice: Not done.

44
00:02:54,386 --> 00:02:57,960
It says this website takes at most that amount in one payment.

45
00:02:58,010 --> 00:02:59,832
No link is created.

46
00:02:59,882 --> 00:03:02,352
Now picture the last minutes of a busy day.

47
00:03:02,402 --> 00:03:04,560
Five buyers press Pay together.

48
00:03:04,610 --> 00:03:08,352
Four charges fit, and go through one after another.

49
00:03:08,402 --> 00:03:10,224
The fifth no longer fits.

50
00:03:10,274 --> 00:03:16,560
Its payment fails with a plain message: the website has reached its limit for today, and no money was taken.

51
00:03:16,610 --> 00:03:18,432
What does this protect?

52
00:03:18,482 --> 00:03:23,424
It protects the bank from a stolen account that tries to move a fortune in one payment.

53
00:03:23,474 --> 00:03:27,912
It protects the merchant from a busy day that quietly goes over the deal.

54
00:03:27,962 --> 00:03:34,560
And it protects buyers: when a limit stops a payment, it stops it before their card is charged, never after.

55
00:03:34,610 --> 00:03:36,432
Back to the concert hall.

56
00:03:36,482 --> 00:03:42,768
However many clerks, however many fans at once, every seat is sold exactly once.

57
00:03:42,818 --> 00:03:46,632
Next: how the system is installed, starting with its keys.
